<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Women's March on Versailles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Black March, October Days, March on Versailles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Women's March on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, also called the October Days, was one of the earliest and most significant events of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. On 5 October 1789 market women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, rioting over the high price of bread, grew into a crowd of thousands that seized weapons at the city hall and marched on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles</a>, followed by fifteen thousand National Guardsmen under Lafayette. Early on 6 October protesters broke into the palace, killing at least two of the royal guards, and the crowd forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and his family to return with them to Paris, where they lived in the Tuileries. Most of the National Constituent Assembly followed, and the march ended the king's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/womens-march-on-versailles.jpg" alt="Women's March on Versailles" width="330" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Versailles,_à_Versailles_5_octobre_1789_-_Restoration.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Fear of famine was ever present among the urban poor at the end of the Ancien Régime, and rumours of a Pacte de Famine to starve them were readily believed. After the storming of the Bastille, price <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and severe shortages were common in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and most workers spent nearly half their income on bread. Calls for a mass demonstration at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> had been made before, notably by the Marquis of Saint-Huruge in August. A banquet held on 1 October by the officers at Versailles for the newly arrived Flanders Regiment was reported in firebrand newspapers as a gluttonous orgy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 5 October a young woman struck a marching drum among market women in the Faubourg Saint-Antoine who were infuriated by the shortage and price of bread. More women joined from other markets, many with kitchen blades and makeshift weapons. At the Hôtel de Ville the crowd, perhaps as many as ten thousand, demanded bread and arms. Their aims widened to bringing the king, the court and the Assembly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. They travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in about six hours, dragging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> from the city hall.</p><p>At Versailles Maillard addressed the Assembly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a> spoke in support of the women. A delegation of six women met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, who promised food. At about six the next morning protesters found a small palace gate unguarded and entered. A guard was killed and beheaded, a second was struck down wounded, and at least one more was killed. Lafayette reconciled the former French Guards with the royal guards, and appeared with the king on a balcony, where the cry of Vive le Roi went up. He then brought out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> and kissed her hand.</p><p>At about one o'clock on 6 October the crowd, grown to over sixty thousand, escorted the royal family and a hundred deputies back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, a journey of about nine hours, some marchers bearing pikes with the heads of the guards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The royal family settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace">Tuileries Palace</a>, where the king reportedly asked for a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a>. The National Constituent Assembly followed within two weeks. Some fifty-six monarchien deputies stayed away, and many, like Jean Joseph Mounier, left the country. Suspicion fell on Louis Philippe II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, as an instigator, but an investigation proved nothing. Historians describe the march as a defining moment of the Revolution, which ended the king's independence and made no further open resistance by him possible.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Women's March on Versailles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Women's March on Versailles</b> on 5 October 1789, women presented a petition to the National Assembly proposing a decree giving women equal rights, and in 1790 Nicolas de Condorcet and Etta Palm d'Aelders unsuccessfully called on the Assembly to extend civil and political rights to women.</blockquote><a href="/work/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The royal family left on 6 October 1789 after the <b>Women's March on Versailles</b>, and the palace was closed.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
